          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the clinical significance of a completely normal 12-lead ECG in a patien</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the clinical significance of a completely normal 12-lead ECG in a patient suspected of having established heart failure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It confirms that the heart failure is well compensated</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A normal ECG does not indicate compensation. Even well-compensated heart failure typically shows abnormalities such as LVH, atrial fibrillation, or conduction defects.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It suggests diastolic dysfunction rather than systolic dysfunction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both systolic and diastolic heart failure can produce ECG abnormalities. A normal tracing does not reliably distinguish between the two types.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It should prompt the clinician to question whether heart failure is truly present</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Established heart failure almost always leaves some mark on the ECG — LVH, AF, bundle branch block, or Q waves — so a completely normal tracing should trigger diagnostic re-evaluation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It rules out an arrhythmic cause but not a structural cause</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The ECG detects both rhythm abnormalities and structural changes such as hypertrophy and prior infarction. A normal ECG is not limited to ruling out arrhythmias alone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The video emphasised that established heart failure almost always leaves a mark on the ECG. A completely normal tracing should prompt the clinician to question whether the diagnosis is correct."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. A truly normal ECG is unusual in established heart failure and should make you reconsider the diagnosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
