          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What determines whether a congenital heart defect presents with cyanosis or with</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What determines whether a congenital heart defect presents with cyanosis or without cyanosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The size of the structural defect</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Defect size affects the severity and volume of the shunt, but it is the direction of blood flow — not the size — that determines whether deoxygenated blood reaches the systemic circulation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The direction of the blood shunt</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Left-to-right shunts recirculate oxygenated blood and remain acyanotic. Right-to-left shunts allow deoxygenated blood to bypass the lungs and enter the systemic circulation, producing cyanosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The patient's age at the time of presentation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Age may influence when symptoms manifest, but it is the shunt direction — whether deoxygenated blood enters the systemic circulation — that determines cyanosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Whether the defect involves a valve or a septum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both valvular and septal defects can be cyanotic or acyanotic. The classification depends on whether deoxygenated blood reaches the systemic circulation, not on which structure is affected.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The video explained that the direction of the shunt is the key determinant: left-to-right shunts stay acyanotic, while right-to-left shunts allow deoxygenated blood into the systemic circulation, causing cyanosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The single question 'which way is blood shunting?' is the key that unlocks the cyanotic versus acyanotic classification."
                }
              ],
